var tafsirData_74 = {
  "1": "\"O you covered up ˹in your clothes˺!\" opens Surah al-Muddaththir with a call parallel to “O you wrapped in your clothes!” The address is again intimate to the Prophet, now covered.\n\n“O you covered up in your clothes!” translates “ya ayyuha al-muddaththir,” O cloaked one. Covered indicates the Prophet enveloped, perhaps at night seeking tranquility after the weighty revelation and the reminder that would humble a mountain. The Lord who said “by the grace of your Lord you are not insane” and “you are of outstanding character” now gently awakens the covered to action, as with the Pen’s oath.\n\nThis follows the easing of night standing and the call to take the Right Way to your Lord, showing that covering’s rest is not for indulgence until you face your Day, but for arising and warning.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as mundane. Covered is affectionate preparation, as with wrapped, to stand, recite, and devote.\n\nFor believers, being covered at night is also your state before standing half the night or less or more, before reciting properly. Respond as the Prophet did, arise and warn, revere your Lord, purify garments, shun idols, do not expect more in return, and persevere for your Lord, so covering becomes the prelude to warning, not to the raging Flame that summons hoarders.\n\nExtra: Covered up also shows the Prophet’s need for reassurance after being told to stand half the night, after weighty revelation, after being mocked for seeking to please wives, after hearts faltered and were invited to turn, and after being hurt like Moses. Covering is not laziness, but the human pause before arising. The Lord who knows whether you speak secretly or openly, who is All-Subtle, All-Aware, addresses the covered tenderly, not as despicable oath-taker, but as beloved, so arise and warn becomes an act of love, not burden. For you who are covered at night, arise similarly, recite properly, and warn your family gently, protecting them from Fire.",
  "2": "\"Arise and warn ˹all˺.\" commands the covered one to rise from covering into mission. The instruction is the core of prophethood after wrapping and covering.\n\n“Arise and warn all” translates “qum fa-andhir,” stand and warn, as with “Stand all night in prayer except a little” turned wrapping into standing for prayer, now covering into standing for warning. Warn, or “andhir,” is to inform of danger before painful punishment, as Noah warned his people before painful punishment, as the keepers ask “Did a warner not come?” and as the reminder to the whole world warns.\n\nAll indicates universal warning, to humankind as Hell is a warning to humankind, to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag, as with the Pen’s writing for all and the Ark as reminder for attentive ears.\n\nThis follows covered up to show that covering’s comfort is for arising, not for persisting in the despicable’s ancient fables.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit warning to threats. Warning includes the Gardens held in honour for the properly observant and the right-hand’s “Read my record” joy.\n\nFor practice, arise and warn in your sphere, protect yourselves and families from Fire whose fuel is people and stones, give rightful share, and be true to trusts, so the difficult Day far from easy for disbelievers is not difficult for those you warned, and the shrouded becomes the shining face looking at its Lord.\n\nMore: Arise and warn also includes warning of the difficult Day far from easy for disbelievers, the Trumpet, the one created alone with wealth and children, the Hell that does not let live, the nineteen keepers, the moon, night, day, and Hell as mightiest catastrophe. Warning all is universal, as the Reminder is to the whole world, the Ark to attentive ears, and the Pen to what everyone writes. Warning all requires purifying garments and shunning idols, so the covered who arises is pure. For the community, arise and warn means stand in solid ranks, give rightful share, and walk upright, so warning is not ancient fables but Right Guidance that leads to Gardens where fruit hangs within reach.",
  "3": "\"Revere your Lord ˹alone˺.\" is the first content of warning after arising. The command is to magnify Allah.\n\n“Revere your Lord alone” translates “wa rabbaka fa-kabbir,” and your Lord magnify. Takbir is to declare greatness, as with “So glorify the Name of your Lord, the Greatest” that closes Inevitable surahs, with Blessed is the One in Whose Hands rests all authority and He is Most Capable, and with the Lord of east and west, no god except Him, take Him as Trustee. Revering magnifies the One who created seven heavens one above another without flaw where sight returns frustrated, who holds birds, who smooths earth and spreads pathways.\n\nAlone indicates exclusive reverence, not associating mate or offspring, not seeking refuge with jinn, not invoking anyone besides Him in places of worship, not taking wealth as lord that diverts.\n\nThis follows arise and warn to show what to warn about first, revering the Greatest, before purifying garments and shunning idols.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to reduce revering to saying “Allahu Akbar” without living it. Revering includes standing half the night, being patient with what they say, departing courteously, and leaving deniers to Allah.\n\nFor daily reverence, magnify your Lord when you see the sky like molten brass will be, when you hear the Trumpet will be sounded as difficult Day, and when you see the garden reduced to ashes while they slept, so revering becomes the weight that steadies the weighty revelation soon sent, and the Pen writes your reverence as the submitted who sought the right course, not the deviant fuel for Hell.\n\nAdditional: Revere your Lord alone also counters the despicable’s ancient fablesเพราะ blessed with wealth and the elite’s urging not to abandon idols Wadd and Nasr. Revering magnifies the Greatest whose Name is to be glorified, the Blessed in Whose Hands rests all authority, Most Capable, who created death and life to test. Revering is not merely saying Allahu Akbar, but living it, shunning idols, purifying garments, not expecting more, and persevering for your Lord. That reverence makes the weighty revelation light, the night worship impactful, and the difficult Day eased for the reverent, who are among the righteous whose Gardens of Bliss are with their Lord.",
  "4": "\"Purify your garments.\" commands inner and outer purity after revering the Lord.\n\n“Purify your garments” translates “wa thiyabaka fa-tahhir,” and your clothes purify. Garments include physical clothes to be clean for prayer that is more impactful at night and properly observed by the excepted, and metaphorical character, as with outstanding character and never-ending reward. Purify indicates cleansing from impurity, like the well-preserved Record touched only by purified angels, and like guarding chastity and protecting families.\n\nThis follows revere your Lord to show that reverence requires purity, and precedes shun idols, showing purity from shirk.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit purification to laundry. Garments’ purification is comprehensive, as with purifying the soul that is tested with impurity of halu‘ distress and withholding.\n\nFor practice, purify garments for prayer, for Friday when you proceed diligently and leave business, for night standing, and for conveying the truth from Allah, so the covered who arises is pure, and the difficult Day far from easy for disbelievers is not difficult for the purified, whose faces will be bright looking at their Lord, and whose Gardens are held in honour where no choking food is.\n\nFurther: Purify your garments also means purify your heart from the halu‘ distress and withholding, from the withholder’s transgression, and from the worthless planks that impress. Purification is both wudu and character, like the well-preserved Record touched only by purified angels and the pen that writes truthfully. Purified garments are needed to stand before the Lord of east and west, to be among those who pray consistently and properly observant, whose Gardens are held in honour. For the arising warner, purified garments ensure that warning is not tainted by desire for more in return, but is for your Lord alone, persevered with beautiful patience.\n\nEven the wrapped who will warn needs purified garments, as the forthcoming warning of Hell that does not let live, that scorches skin, overseen by nineteen, requires purity to be heard. Purified garments also mean the pen writes cleanly, the Record remains well-preserved, and the reminder to the whole world is not stained by idol impurity, so the difficult Day is met with honour, not disgrace.",
  "5": "\"˹Continue to˺ shun idols.\" orders separation from impurity after purifying garments. The command is to abandon what pollutes.\n\n“Continue to shun idols” translates “wa al-rujza fa-hjur,” and defilement, or “rujz,” forsake, as with shunning the abomination of idols Wadd, Suwā‘, Yaghūth, Ya‘ūq, Nasr urged not to abandon, and the idols that make the elite’s wealth increase only in loss. Rujz is impurity that soils garments, like ancient fables dismissal, pure magic charge, and withholder of good.\n\nShun, or “hajr,” is to leave, depart courteously as you depart from deniers, to be patient with what they say, to leave them to Me, the deniers of luxury. Shunning idols is the practical form of “He is the Lord of east and west, there is no god except Him, so take Him alone as Trustee.”\n\nThis follows purify garments to show that purification includes removing idol impurity.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think shunning idols means breaking kindly, fair relations with those who do not fight. Shunning is of idols and their worship, not of kindly dealing with peaceful people.\n\nFor the covered who arises to warn, shunning idols is core warning, worship Allah alone, fear Him, obey me as Noah said, and believe in Allah, His Messenger, and the Light, so you are not among those who say “We will never obey anyone against you” to fellow disbelievers, but among the submitted who sought right course and whose Gardens are held in honour, where no idol’s fuel feeds Hell.\n\nExtra: Shun idols, or “rujz,” also shuns the modern idols of merchandise fanfare that drew crowds from the pulpit, honour that expels inferior, and wealth that says ancient fables. Idols are whatever you take alongside Allah, the King, Holy, whose Places of worship are only for Him. Shunning is Beautiful patience with what they say and courteous departure from deniers, leaving luxury deniers to Him for a little while. Shunning does not mean cursing people kindly treated like those who neither fight nor drive you out, but rejecting their idols. That shunning keeps garments purified and warning clear, so the difficult Day remains far from easy for the idolaters, not for you.",
  "6": "\"Do not do a favour expecting more ˹in return˺.\" warns the warner after shunning idols. The counsel is about sincerity in giving.\n\n“Do not do a favour expecting more in return” translates “wa la tamnun tastakthir,” do not do kindness to seek increase. Tamnun is to do a favour and remind or expect multiplication for worldly gain, as with the despicable’s favour that withholds good and expects more, and the garden owners’ harvest for all without “Allah willing.” Expecting more is to treat da‘wah, charity, and teaching as merchandise fanfare that draws crowds, better than what is with Allah, but it is not.\n\nThis follows shun idols to show that even good acts lose value if done for increase, and precedes persevere for your Lord, showing the correct motive.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to discourage expecting Allah’s increase. Expecting from Allah is commanded, as with the good loan multiplied and forgiven, and whatever good you send forth you will find with Allah better and greater. The prohibition is expecting more from people.\n\nFor the arising warner, do not warn to be called not insane, nor to gain wealth and children, nor to be honoured to expel inferior, but for your Lord. For believers, give rightful share, lend good loan, make room, and protect families without expecting worldly more, so favour becomes sincere, and the difficult Day far from easy for disbelievers becomes easy for the sincere, whose never-ending reward is with the Lord of infinite bounty who grants it to whoever He wills.\n\nMore: Do not do a favour expecting more also corrects the garden owners’ expectation to harvest all early for more, the hypocrites’ oaths as shields expecting more status, and the despicable’s withholder expecting more through withholding. The Prophet, who will have a never-ending reward not cut off, is told not to seek human more, but Allah’s more, the great reward with Allah, the Gardens of Bliss, the excellent provision. For believers, this means giving rightful share without reminding, lending good loan without expecting worldly return, and making room without expecting seat, so favour is for your Lord, whose planning is flawless and who delays for a while but rewards better and greater.",
  "7": "\"And persevere for ˹the sake of˺ your Lord.\" closes the opening commands to the covered after favour without expecting more. The charge is perseverance.\n\n“And persevere for the sake of your Lord” translates “wa li-rabbika fa-sbir,” and for your Lord be patient. Patience here is the beautiful patience commanded, enduring denial with beautiful patience while they see Day as far but We see as near, not like Jonah in distress, and being patient with what they say and departing courteously. For your Lord indicates motive, as with revering your Lord, purifying for Him, shunning idols for Him, not for merchandise or honour. It mirrors “We will soon send upon you a weighty revelation” which requires night impact and measured recitation.\n\nThis follows not doing favour for more, showing that true favour’s reward is with Allah, so patience is possible.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think perseverance is passive. Perseverance includes standing half the night, reciting properly, remembering wholeheartedly, taking Trustee, and warning, all for your Lord.\n\nFor the covered who arises and warns, perseverance sustains when the Trumpet will be sounded as difficult Day far from easy, when the one I created all by Myself with wealth and children will be left to Me, and when the despicable’s ancient fables is said. Persevering for your Lord places you among those who pray consistently, give rightful share, and are properly observant, whose Gardens are held in honour, where perseverance is met with “Eat and drink joyfully for what you did in days gone by.”\n\nFurther: Persevere for your Lord also embraces the entire opening sequence: wrapped, arise, revere, purify, shun idols, not expecting more. Perseverance is for your Lord, not for honour to expel inferior or merchandise fanfare. It is the beautiful patience with the Lord’s decree, not like Jonah in distress who would have been cast blameworthy without grace then chosen and made righteous. Persevering for your Lord makes the Trumpet’s difficult Day not difficult for you, because you persevered through night standing, measured recitation, and weighty carrying, so the never-ending reward and outstanding character remain yours, and the Gardens held in honour become your perseverance’s home.",
  "8": "\"˹For˺ when the Trumpet will be sounded,\" introduces the difficult Day that requires reverence, purification, shunning idols, favour without expecting more, and perseverance. The Trumpet marks the Inevitable.\n\n“For when the Trumpet will be sounded” translates “fa-idha nuqira fi al-naqur,” when it is blown in the Horn, as with the Trumpet blown with one blast after which the Inevitable Event will have come to pass, earth and mountains lifted and crushed, sky torn frail, angels on sides, Throne borne by eight, presentation with no secrets hidden. Trumpet, or “naqur,” is the Horn, like the Ark’s reminder and the pen’s writing, a signal that cuts through indulgence and amusement until they face their Day.\n\nThis follows persevere for your Lord to show what perseverance prepares for, and precedes difficult Day far from easy for disbelievers.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat Trumpet as mythic. It is the appointed time that can never be delayed, the Day fifty thousand years they see as far.\n\nFor practice, persevere for your Lord so the Trumpet that will be sounded as difficult for disbelievers is not difficult for you, but the beginning of the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach, where the covered who arose and warned is held in honour, and where the shackles, raging Fire, and choking food in store for luxury deniers are not yours but for those left to Me whom you bore with for a little while.\n\nAdditional: The Trumpet will be sounded also signals that the time to be patient with what they say and to leave luxury deniers for a little while is limited. Little while is until the Trumpet, as with “let them indulge and amuse until they face their Day” and “gradually draw them in ways they cannot comprehend, I delay for a while.” The Trumpet is the end of little while, the start of the Day fifty thousand years. The covered who perseveres knows this, so night standing is preparation. For the community, the Trumpet warns that the difficult Day far from easy is coming, so arise and warn now, revere, purify, shun, and persevere, so the Trumpet finds you among those whose light shines ahead, not among those who indulge.",
  "9": "\"that will ˹truly˺ be a difficult Day—\" describes the Trumpet’s aftermath after perseverance is commanded. The Day is characterized.\n\n“that will truly be a difficult Day—” translates “fa-dhalika yawma’idhin yawmun ‘asir,” that then is a day hard, difficult, as with the difficult Day far from easy for the disbelievers, the Day the disbelievers’ faces become gloomy, the Day no close friend will ask, the Day the wicked wish to ransom with everyone on earth. Difficult, or “‘asir,” is hard, severe, as with severe account for rebellious societies, horrible punishment tasted, and shackles and dunes. Truly emphasizes certainty, as with the absolute truth and the inevitable.\n\nThis follows Trumpet sounded to show consequence, and precedes far from easy for disbelievers, specifying for whom it is difficult.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think difficult means inescapable for all, including the excepted who pray and give. Difficult is far from easy for disbelievers, but for the properly observant held in honour it is the Day their light shines ahead and they are told to eat and drink joyfully.\n\nFor the covered who arises, difficulty underscores why revering, purifying, shunning idols, not expecting more, and persevering are needed now, so the difficult Day becomes, for the sincere, the Day where weighty revelation’s night standing bears fruit as Gardens of Bliss with the Lord, where difficulty is replaced by honour and near fruit.\n\nMore: A difficult Day, or “‘asir,” also describes the garden’s morning when owners found ashes, the seven nights and eight days of wind for ‘Ad, and the stern grip for Pharaoh. Difficult is hard, heavy, as with the weighty revelation soon sent. Difficult truly, or “fa-dhalika,” emphasizes that no powerless force will help, no oaths bind until the Day, no scripture reads whatever you choose. Difficult is relieved only by what is with Allah better than amusement and merchandise, Best Provider, and by the good sent forth found with Allah better and greater. To make it not difficult, take the Right Way to your Lord now, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, and lend good loan, so difficulty is for luxury deniers, not for you.",
  "10": "\"far from easy for the disbelievers.\" specifies for whom the difficult Day is hard. The verse completes the description.\n\n“far from easy for the disbelievers” translates “‘ala al-kafirina ghayru yasir,” upon the disbelievers not easy. Ghayru yasir is not eased, not made light, as with the way out and provision from sources never imagined for the mindful, and ease after hardship, which are eased for the mindful, but not for those who persist like Pharaoh disobeying, garden owners withholding, and brute saying ancient fables.\n\nThis follows difficult Day to distinguish, and precedes “And leave to me the one I created all by Myself,” showing that the disbelievers’ difficulty includes being left to Allah’s handling, as with the luxury deniers left to Him.\n\nDisbelievers here are those who turned backs and hoarded, whose wealth did not benefit, whose authority was stripped, who were told seize and shackle then burn and chain.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think “far from easy” means disbelievers have no chance now. Until the Trumpet is sounded, the Reminder is still a reminder, whoever wills may take the Right Way.\n\nFor reflection, do not be among those for whom it is far from easy. Be among those who take the Right Way now, who recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so the difficult Day is eased for you as He makes your matters easy for the mindful, and the far from easy becomes near fruit within reach in the Gardens held in honour.\n\nExtra: Far from easy for the disbelievers also distinguishes the excepted who pray consistently, give rightful share, believe firmly, fear punishment, guard chastity, keep trusts, testify honestly, and observe prayers. Far from easy is the same as the raging Flame that rips scalps, the Blaze that almost bursts, and the choking food. Easy for the disbelievers would be if everyone on earth could ransom, but “But no! There will certainly be a raging Flame” denies. For the disbelievers, the Day is not eased as it is for the mindful where He will make a way out, provide from sources never imagined, be sufficient, achieve Will, set destiny, and after hardship bring ease. Choose the eased path, not the far from easy.",
  "11": "“And leave to me ˹O Prophet˺ the one I created all by Myself,” opens the account of the man whom the surah addresses without naming, and the commentators identify him and unfold the verse. “And leave to me O Prophet the one I created all by Myself,” or “tharnee wa man khalaqtu waheeda.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse refers to al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, the chief of the Makhzum clan of Quraysh, a man of great wealth and influence, and the believer is taught that God addressed the Prophet with the command to leave this man to Him, for the One who created him alone is the One who will deal with him alone, and the messenger is not to be troubled by the defiance of the defiant, for the defiant is in the hand of the One who made him. Al-Tabari notes the word “waheed” — “alone,” “all by Myself” — and the believer is taught that the man was created by God alone, without the help of any of the partners that the idolaters claimed, and that the one who is created alone is the one who will be judged alone, and that the believer is taught to leave the defiant to the Lord of the defiant.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the verse for the believer who is troubled by the mockers, for the verse tells the Prophet, and through him every believer, to leave the mocker to God, and the believer is taught that the believer's task is the delivery of the message and not the punishment of the mockers, and that the punishment is the affair of the Lord who created the mocker and will judge him, and that the leaving of the mocker to God is the peace of the believer and the terror of the mocker. The verse also teaches the believer the solitude of the judgment, for the man who was created alone will stand alone, and the believer is taught that the wealth and the influence of the defiant will not accompany him to the judgment, and that the believer is taught to prepare for the solitude by the company of the good deeds. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to leave the mockers to God, and to know that the One who created them alone will judge them alone, and that the believer is taught to occupy himself with the truth and to leave the falsehood to its end.",
  "12": "“and granted him abundant wealth,” begins the enumeration of what God had given to the man, and the verse names the first of his gifts. “And granted him abundant wealth,” or “wa ja'altu lahu malan mamdooda.” Ibn Kathir explains that God had given al-Walid abundant and extended wealth, and the believer is taught that the wealth of the defiant is the gift of God, and that the gift which is not met with gratitude becomes the proof against its owner, and that the wealth of al-Walid, which was “mamdood” — extended, stretching — was the very thing that he would leave behind when the judgment came. Al-Tabari notes that the wealth was abundant and continuous, and the believer is taught that the abundance of the wealth is the test of the wealth, and that the one who is given the abundant wealth is tested whether he will give thanks or grow proud, and that the proud wealth is the wealth that testifies against its owner.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the enumeration, for the surah is about to list the gifts of the man — the wealth, the children, the ease — and the believer is taught that the gifts are the tests, and that the man who was given all and gave no thanks is the man whose gifts became his ruin, and that the believer is taught to receive the gifts of God with the gratitude that turns the gift into the blessing, and to know that the gift which is received with the pride is the gift which is turned into the punishment. The verse also teaches the believer the source of the wealth, for the wealth is the grant of God, and the believer is taught to see his own wealth as the grant and not the earning, and to know that the one who sees the wealth as his own has already lost it, while the one who sees it as the grant has kept it. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to thank God for the wealth and to spend it in the obedience, and to know that the abundant wealth of the defiant is the abundant witness against the defiant.",
  "13": "“and children always by his side,” continues the enumeration with the children, and the verse names the second of the man's gifts. “And children always by his side,” or “wa baneena shuhooda.” Ibn Kathir explains that God had given al-Walid sons who were present with him, never absent, and the believer is taught that the children are among the greatest of the gifts, and that the children who are “shuhood” — present, attending — are the comfort of the father, and that the children of the defiant were the comfort that he would lose, and that the gift of the children is the test of the children, and that the father who is given the children is tested whether he raises them in the faith or in the defiance. Al-Tabari notes the word “shuhood” — “present,” “witnessing” — and the believer is taught that the children who are present are the witnesses of the father, and that the father is witnessed by his children in his deeds, and that the believer is taught to make his children the witnesses of his good.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the children, for the children are the extension of the man, and the believer is taught that the children who are raised in the faith are the extension of the father's good, while the children who are raised in the defiance are the extension of the father's ruin, and that the father is responsible for the direction of the extension, and that the believer is taught to raise his children for the Hereafter, and to know that the children who are by his side in this world will either be by his side or against him in the next. The verse also teaches the believer the vanity of the worldly gifts, for the children who were by al-Walid's side could not accompany him to the judgment, and the believer is taught to build the bond of the faith with his children, for the bond of the faith is the bond that endures while the bond of the blood is the bond that the judgment may sever. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to thank God for the children and to raise them for God, and to know that the children who are present in this world are the trust that will be asked about in the next.",
  "14": "“and made life very easy for him.” completes the enumeration with the ease, and the verse names the third of the man's gifts. “And made life very easy for him,” or “wa mahhadtu lahu tamheeda.” Ibn Kathir explains that God had spread out the life of al-Walid and made it smooth and easy, and the believer is taught that the ease of the life is the test of the ease, and that the one whose life is made smooth is tested whether he will remember the One who smoothed it or forget Him in the smoothness, and that the ease of the defiant was the very thing that hardened his heart, and that the believer is taught to fear the ease that hardens. Al-Tabari notes the word “tamheed” — “spreading,” “smoothing” — and the believer is taught that the life which is spread out is the life which is most dangerous, for the smooth path is the path on which the traveler forgets to watch his step, and the believer is taught to watch his step on the smooth path.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the three gifts, for the wealth and the children and the ease are the three things that men desire, and the man was given all three and was ruined by them, and the believer is taught that the ruin of the gifted is the ruin of the gratitude that was not given, and that the gifts of God are the tests that the heart either passes by the thanks or fails by the pride, and that the believer is taught to pass the test by the thanks, and to know that the thanks is the keeping of the gift. The verse also teaches the believer the danger of the ease, for the ease which is not met with the remembrance is the ease which becomes the forgetfulness, and the believer is taught to fill the ease with the remembrance, and to know that the one who remembers in the ease is the one whom God remembers in the hardship. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the smooth life as he fears the hard, and to know that the ease which is received with the gratitude is the mercy, while the ease which is received with the forgetfulness is the trial.",
  "15": "“Yet he is hungry for more.” states the response of the man to his gifts, and the verse exposes the greed that the abundance had produced. “Yet he is hungry for more,” or “thumma yatma'u an azeed.” Ibn Kathir explains that al-Walid, despite all that God had given him, was greedy for more, and the believer is taught that the greed of the heart is the disease that the abundance does not cure, and that the one who is given the wealth and the children and the ease and still hungers has a hunger that no gift can fill, and that the hunger for the world is the hunger that grows with the feeding. Al-Tabari notes the word “yatma'” — “he craves,” “he hopes greedily” — and the believer is taught that the craving of the defiant is the craving that never rests, and that the one who craves the more is the one who is never satisfied, and that the satisfaction is the gift of the heart and not of the hand.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the greed, for the verse teaches the believer that the love of the world is the disease that the world cannot cure, and that the one who is given the world and hungers for more has made the world his god, and that the god of the world is the god that consumes its worshipper, and that the believer is taught to cure the greed by the contentment, and to know that the contentment is the wealth that no thief can steal. The verse also teaches the believer the difference between the desire and the greed, for the desire for the lawful is the desire that is satisfied, while the greed for the more is the greed that is inflamed, and the believer is taught to desire with the limits and to flee the greed, and to know that the one who hungers for the more is the one who will be given the less in the Hereafter. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to make the gratitude the answer to the gifts, and to know that the one who answers the gifts with the greed has answered the mercy with the defiance, and that the answer is the ruin that the next verses record.",
  "16": "“But no! ˹For˺ he has been truly stubborn with Our revelations.” states the deeper disease beneath the greed, and the verse names the stubbornness that the abundance had revealed. “But no! For he has been truly stubborn with Our revelations,” or “kalla innahu kana li-ayatina 'aneeda.” Ibn Kathir explains that the man was not merely greedy but stubborn against the signs of God, and the believer is taught that the greed for the world is the fruit of the stubbornness against the truth, and that the one who refuses the signs of God is the one whose heart is filled with the hunger for the world, and that the stubbornness is the root and the greed is the branch. Al-Tabari notes the word “'aneed” — “stubborn,” “obstinate” — and the believer is taught that the stubbornness is the refusal that has become a habit, and that the one who is stubborn against the signs has set his will against the will of God, and that the setting of the will against God is the ruin of the will.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the stubbornness, for the verse teaches the believer that the danger of the abundance is the stubbornness it breeds, and that the man who is given the world and refuses the truth has been tested and has failed, and that the failure is the stubbornness that the verse names, and that the believer is taught to guard his heart against the stubbornness, and to receive the signs of God with the softness that the truth deserves. The verse also teaches the believer the difference between the rejection and the stubbornness, for the rejection is the refusal of the truth, while the stubbornness is the refusal that has become the nature, and the believer is taught to uproot the rejection before it becomes the stubbornness, and to know that the stubbornness is the door that closes against the mercy. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the stubbornness, and to keep his heart soft to the signs of God, and to know that the softness of the heart is the safety from the fate that the surah records.",
  "17": "“I will make his fate unbearable,” states the punishment that the stubbornness has earned, and the verse turns from the description of the man to the threat against him. “I will make his fate unbearable,” or “sa-urhiquhu sa'ooda.” Ibn Kathir explains that the word “sa'ood” refers to a mountain of fire in Hell that the man will be made to climb, and the believer is taught that the punishment of the stubborn is the punishment of the ascent, the endless climbing of the mountain of fire, and that the one who made his life easy in the world will find his fate hard in the next, and that the ease which was not thanked becomes the hardship that does not end. Al-Tabari notes the interpretation of “sa'ood” as the mountain of fire, and the believer is taught that the punishment of the Hereafter is the punishment that is fitted to the ease of this world, and that the one who climbed the world with ease will climb the fire with pain.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the fitting punishment, for the verse teaches the believer that the punishment of the Hereafter answers the sin of this world, and that the man whose life was smoothed and spread was given the ascent that is the opposite of the ease, and that the believer is taught to use the ease of this world for the obedience, and to know that the ease which is spent in the forgetfulness becomes the hardship of the ascent. The verse also teaches the believer the certainty of the threat, for the word “sa” — “I will” — is the word of the certain future, and the believer is taught that the threat of God is as certain as the promise of God, and that the one who is threatened is the one who will be taken, and that the believer is taught to take the threat as the mercy that warns before the taking. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the ascent of the fire, and to spend his ease in the obedience, and to know that the one whose world is easy and whose obedience is full is the one whose next is the Garden and not the ascent.",
  "18": "“for he contemplated and determined ˹a degrading label for the Quran˺.” turns to the plotting of the man, and the verse records the thought that the stubbornness produced. “For he contemplated and determined a degrading label for the Quran,” or “innahu fakkara wa qaddara.” Ibn Kathir explains that al-Walid contemplated and calculated what to say about the Quran, and the believer is taught that the rejection of the truth is not always the instant refusal but often the careful calculation, and that the man sat and thought and determined what label to attach to the Quran, and that the calculation of the rejecter is the worst of his deeds, for the one who thinks and then rejects has rejected with the full weight of his mind. Al-Tabari notes that the verse refers to the well-known occasion when the Quraysh sent al-Walid to hear the Prophet recite and then to judge what the Quran was, and he contemplated and then pronounced his verdict, and the believer is taught that the verdict was the verdict of the one who had heard and calculated and then refused.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the contemplation, for the verse teaches the believer that the rejection which follows the contemplation is the rejection which is the most guilty, and that the man who thought about the Quran and then labeled it was the man who had seen the truth and turned from it with the eyes open, and that the believer is taught to contemplate the truth in order to accept it, and to know that the contemplation which is used to refuse is the contemplation that the verse curses. The verse also teaches the believer the difference between the thought and the faith, for the thought can be the servant of the desire, and the believer is taught to let his thought serve the truth, and to know that the mind which is used to build the rejection is the mind which has been turned against its own good. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to contemplate the Quran in order to believe, and to know that the one who contemplates in order to reject has contemplated his own ruin, and that the ruin is the curse that the next verse pronounces.",
  "19": "“May he be condemned! How evil was what he determined!” pronounces the curse upon the man for his determination, and the verse is the first of the two-fold condemnation that the surah records. “May he be condemned! How evil was what he determined!” or “fa-qutila kayfa qaddara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse curses the man and condemns the determination he made, and the believer is taught that the curse of God is the opposite of the mercy of God, and that the one who determined a degrading label for the Quran has determined his own ruin, and that the determination which was made against the truth is the determination which the truth answers with the curse. Al-Tabari notes the word “qutila” — “may he be killed,” “may he be condemned” — and the believer is taught that the word is the word of the ruin, and that the one who plotted against the truth is the one upon whom the ruin is pronounced.\n\nAl-Razi observes the force of the question “how evil was what he determined,” for the verse does not merely curse the man but exposes the evil of his determination, and the believer is taught that the determination of the rejecter is the determination of the evil, and that the evil of the determination is the measure of the evil of the determiner, and that the believer is taught to guard his determinations, for the determination which is made against the truth is the determination which is made against the self. The verse also teaches the believer the seriousness of the word, for the word which is determined against the Quran is the word which is weighed, and the believer is taught to weigh his words before he speaks them, and to know that the word which is spoken against the truth is the word which will testify against its speaker. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the curse of God, and to keep his determinations in the service of the truth, and to know that the one who determines evil is the one upon whom the evil returns.",
  "20": "“May he be condemned even more! How evil was what he determined!” repeats the curse and the condemnation, and the verse doubles the judgment upon the determination. “May he be condemned even more! How evil was what he determined!” or “thumma qutila kayfa qaddara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the curse is repeated, and the believer is taught that the repetition is the emphasis, and that the determination of the man was so evil that the Quran curses it twice, and that the one who is cursed twice is the one whose ruin is doubled, and that the believer is taught to measure the evil of the determination by the number of the curses, and to know that the rejection of the truth is the deed whose evil the Quran underlines. Al-Tabari notes the repetition, and the believer is taught that the repetition of the curse is the sign of the severity of the deed, and that the one who contemplates the truth and rejects it has done the deed that the Quran cannot pass over once.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the doubling, for the verse teaches the believer that the determination against the truth is the sin which is doubled, and that the sin which is doubled is the sin which is doubled in its punishment, and that the believer is taught to fear the sin of the tongue and the thought, and to know that the thought which is turned against the truth is the thought which the Quran curses twice. The verse also teaches the believer the completeness of the exposure, for the verse does not let the determination pass with a single curse but returns to it, and the believer is taught that the truth does not hurry past the falsehood but exposes it fully, and that the exposure of the falsehood is the mercy of the truth to the one who will hear. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let the double curse teach him the weight of the word, and to know that the one who speaks against the truth has spoken the word that the Quran curses, and that the believer is taught to speak the truth and to leave the falsehood to its curse.",
  "21": "“Then he re-contemplated ˹in frustration˺,” continues the account of the man's contemplation, and the verse shows the rejecter returning to his thought. “Then he re-contemplated in frustration,” or “thumma nathara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the man looked again and reconsidered, and the believer is taught that the rejecter of the truth is the one who returns to his rejection, and that the first rejection is followed by the second, and that the returning to the thought is the returning to the sin, and that the one who re-contemplates his rejection has deepened his rejection. Al-Tabari notes the word “nathara” — “he looked,” “he reconsidered” — and the believer is taught that the man looked again at the Quran and at the Prophet, and that his second look was the look that confirmed his first refusal, and that the look which is made to refuse is the look which deepens the refusal.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the returning, for the verse teaches the believer that the rejecter is not at rest in his rejection but returns to it again and again, and that the returning is the sign of the struggle within, and that the struggle which ends in the refusal is the struggle which has been lost, and that the believer is taught to let his struggle end in the acceptance, and to know that the one who returns to the thought and still refuses has refused with the full knowledge of what he refuses. The verse also teaches the believer the picture of the one who would not believe, for the man contemplated and re-contemplated, and the believer is taught that the contemplation which is not ended by the faith is the contemplation which is ended by the frustration, and that the frustration is the mark of the one who saw and refused. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to end his contemplation with the faith, and to know that the one who contemplates and returns to the refusal is the one whose next step is the frown that the next verse records.",
  "22": "“then frowned and scowled,” records the outward sign of the inward refusal, and the verse shows the face of the rejecter after his contemplation. “Then frowned and scowled,” or “thumma 'abasa wa basara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the man frowned and scowled, and the believer is taught that the face is the mirror of the heart, and that the one who has refused the truth in his heart shows the refusal on his face, and that the frown and the scowl are the marks of the one who has seen the truth and hated it, and that the believer is taught to guard his face as he guards his heart, and to know that the face which frowns at the truth is the face of the heart which has refused it. Al-Tabari notes the two words — “'abasa” and “basara” — and the believer is taught that the frown and the scowl are the two degrees of the same refusal, and that the face which is turned against the truth is the face which has turned against the light.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the face, for the verse teaches the believer that the rejection of the truth is not a hidden thing, and that the heart which refuses shows its refusal in the face, and that the believer is taught to meet the truth with the face of the acceptance, and to know that the face which brightens at the truth is the face of the heart which has accepted it, and that the believer is taught to let his face testify to his heart. The verse also teaches the believer the picture of the rejecter, for the man who contemplated the Quran and then frowned is the man whose contemplation ended in the hatred, and the believer is taught to fear the end of the contemplation which is not ended by the faith, and to know that the end is the frown and the scowl and the turning away that follows. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to receive the truth with the open face, and to know that the frown at the truth is the sign of the ruin, and that the believer is taught to let his face be the face of the one who loves the light.",
  "23": "“then turned his back ˹on the truth˺ and acted arrogantly,” completes the picture of the rejection, and the verse shows the turning away and the arrogance that followed the frown. “Then turned his back on the truth and acted arrogantly,” or “thumma adbara wastakbara.” Ibn Kathir explains that the man turned his back on the truth and grew arrogant, and the believer is taught that the refusal of the truth proceeds by degrees — the contemplation, then the frown, then the turning away, then the arrogance — and that the degrees are the descent of the heart, and that the one who begins with the doubt and does not stop ends in the arrogance, and that the believer is taught to stop the descent at its first degree. Al-Tabari notes the two words — “adbara” and “istakbara” — and the believer is taught that the turning of the back is the abandonment of the truth, and that the arrogance is the claim of the self against the truth, and that the two together are the full rejection.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the descent, for the verse teaches the believer the path of the ruin, and that the ruin is not a single step but a descent, and that the believer is taught to watch the first step of the descent, and to know that the one who does not stop the doubt stops only at the arrogance, and that the arrogance is the end of the descent and the beginning of the punishment. The verse also teaches the believer the difference between the believer and the rejecter, for the believer turns his face to the truth and humbles himself, while the rejecter turns his back and grows arrogant, and the believer is taught to turn and to humble, and to know that the turning and the humbling are the two halves of the faith. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the descent of the heart, and to stop at the first doubt, and to know that the one who turns his back on the truth has turned his back on the mercy, and that the turning is the step that the punishment follows.",
  "24": "“saying, ‘This ˹Quran˺ is nothing but magic from the ancients.” records the verdict that the man's contemplation produced, and the verse preserves the label that he attached to the Quran. “Saying, 'This Quran is nothing but magic from the ancients,'” or “fa-qala in hatha illa sihrun yu'thar.” Ibn Kathir explains that al-Walid, after his contemplation, pronounced the Quran to be magic handed down from the ancients, and the believer is taught that the label of magic is the refuge of the one who cannot answer the truth, and that the one who calls the truth magic has confessed that the truth is beyond his power to match, and that the label is the confession of the defeat, and that the believer is taught to recognize the label and to see the confession beneath it. Al-Tabari notes the word “yu'thar” — “handed down,” “related from the ancients” — and the believer is taught that the man called the Quran the inherited magic of the ancients, and that the label was the best that his contemplation could produce, and that the best of the rejecter is the worst of the labels.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the label, for the verse teaches the believer that the truth has always been called magic, and that the label of magic is the old label of the old rejecters, and that the believer is taught to hear the label without being shaken, and to know that the truth which is called magic is the truth which the rejecter cannot match, and that the inability to match is the proof of the truth. The verse also teaches the believer the poverty of the rejection, for the man who was given the wealth and the children and the ease could produce nothing better than the label of magic, and the believer is taught that the rejection of the truth is always poor, and that the wealth of the rejecter cannot buy him a better argument, and that the believer is taught to hold the truth which the rejecter cannot answer. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to expect the label of magic, and to answer it with the truth, and to know that the label is the sign of the truth, for the magic is the name that the defeated give to what they cannot defeat.",
  "25": "“This is no more than the word of a man.’” records the second half of the man's verdict, and the verse completes the label with the reduction of the Quran to human speech. “This is no more than the word of a man,” or “in hatha illa qawlu al-bashar.” Ibn Kathir explains that al-Walid, having called the Quran magic, then called it the word of a man, and the believer is taught that the two labels contradict each other, for the magic is the work of the sorcerer and the word of the man is the work of the human, and the one who cannot decide between the two labels has confessed the confusion of the rejecter, and that the believer is taught to see the contradiction and to know that the rejecter is the one whose arguments fight each other. Al-Tabari notes the contradiction, and the believer is taught that the man who called the Quran both magic and the word of a man was the man whose rejection had no ground, and that the groundless rejection is the rejection that the next verses answer.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the contradiction, for the verse teaches the believer that the rejection of the truth is never coherent, and that the labels of the rejecters are the labels of the confused, and that the believer is taught to hold the truth which is one, and to leave the rejection which is many, and to know that the truth is recognized by its oneness and the falsehood by its contradiction. The verse also teaches the believer the honor of the Quran, for the Quran is not the word of a man but the word of the Lord, and the believer is taught to receive it as the word of the Lord, and to know that the one who reduces the Quran to the word of a man has reduced the Lord to the level of His creatures, and that the reduction is the sin that the surah has been condemning. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to know the Quran as the word of God, and to answer the reduction of the rejecter with the certainty of the believer, and to know that the word of the Lord is not lowered by the label of the rejecter, and that the rejecter is the one who is lowered by his own label.",
  "26": "\"Soon I will burn him in Hell!\" pronounces the requital after the two degrading labels. The threat is direct from Allah, the speaker of “I will make his fate unbearable.”\n\n“Soon I will burn him in Hell!” translates “sa-uslihi saqar,” I will roast him in Saqar, the scorching Fire. Saqar is a name of Hell, like Laza ripping scalps, Jahim, and Blaze that almost bursts, the raging Flame, choking food, pain. Soon, or “sa-,” indicates near, as with soon you and the pagans will see which is mad, soon We will mark his snout, soon We will send weighty, and soon the garden reduced to ashes while they slept. Burn, or “uslihi,” is to make enter and roast, as with “Seize and shackle them, then burn them in Hell, then tie them with chains seventy arms long,” and as with shackles, raging Fire, choking food in store.\n\nThis follows “This is no more than the word of a man” to show that calling revelation human word leads to being entered into Fire, as with those who said ancient fables because blessed with wealth.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think Saqar is mere intimidation. It is real, described next as not letting live or die, scorching skin, overseen by nineteen, appointed as test, mighty catastrophe, warning to humankind.\n\nFor the living who are told to arise and warn, soon burning warns that warning is urgent, not indifferent, as with “leave to Me the one I created all by Myself.” To avoid soon burning, be among those who revere your Lord, purify garments, shun idols, do not expect more, and persevere for your Lord, so soon is not Saqar but Gardens held in honour where the difficult Day is not difficult for you.\n\nMore: Soon I will burn him in Hell also warns the luxurious deniers—people of luxury—left to Me for a little while, whose choking food and painful punishment are in store. Soon is near, as with soon you and the pagans will see which is mad, soon We will mark his snout, and soon the garden reduced to ashes while they slept. Burning in Saqar, which does not let live or die and scorches skin, overseen by nineteen, is the appointed burning for “This Quran is nothing but magic” and “word of a man.” To avoid soon burning, be among those who revere, purify garments, shun idols, and persevere for your Lord, so soon is Gardens of Bliss with your Lord, not Saqar.",
  "27": "\"And what will make you realize what Hell is?\" magnifies Saqar after declaring “Soon I will burn him in Hell.” The question parallels the Inevitable Hour’s magnification.\n\n“And what will make you realize what Hell is?” translates “wa ma adraka ma saqar,” what will inform you what Saqar is? The phrase is used for weighty unseen realities like the Night of Decree, the Inevitable, and the Striking, indicating that realization requires prophetic informing, not imagination, as with “That knowledge is with Allah alone, and I am only a clear warner” for when the threat will come. Saqar’s reality exceeds what the one who contemplated a degrading label can imagine.\n\nThis follows burning to show that burning’s nature is beyond his degraded label, and precedes “It does not let anyone live or die, scorching the skin,” which gives part of the answer.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to answer with speculation about Hell’s location or size. The Quran answers with its effects, not letting live or die, scorching, nineteen keepers, mighty catastrophe, warning.\n\nFor the denier who said “This is nothing but magic from ancients, this is word of a man,” the question shows his degraded label did not make Hell less, it made his realization greater when he sees what he dismissed as human word is actually the word that would humble a mountain.\n\nFor practice, let “what will make you realize what Hell is?” make you realize now, before seeing torment drawing near with gloomy faces. Realize through the Reminder that is for those mindful, the absolute truth, and the weighty revelation, so realization leads to protecting families from Fire whose fuel is people and stones, not to being burned in Saqar.\n\nFurther, what will make you realize what Hell is, or “ma adraka ma saqar,” is the same question as “what will make you realize what the Inevitable and Striking are?” It signals that Saqar’s reality exceeds imagination, as does the garden’s better hoped for, the elevated Garden’s near fruit, and the Day fifty thousand years. Realization now through the Reminder prevents realization then through roaring and fury. For the covered who is told to arise and warn, realizing Saqar now makes warning urgent and sincere, not expecting more in return, so the covered’s warning saves himself and others from needing to realize Saqar by being burned, and instead they realize Gardens held in honour.",
  "28": "\"It does not let anyone live or die,\" describes Saqar’s nature after asking what will make you realize what Hell is. The phrase captures endless torment.\n\n“It does not let anyone live or die” translates “la tubqi wa la tadar,” it leaves nothing and spares nothing, or it does not let live nor let die, as with the garden’s total loss where outcome was total loss, and as with the Fire that does not let live or die for the one burned. Not letting live means no true life, no relief, no Gardens’ bliss, no elevated Garden’s near fruit; not letting die means no escape, no appointed time that never delays therefore ends, unlike worldly punishments like drowning then Fire where drowning was end of worldly, but Hell’s Fire has no end for the persistent.\n\nThis follows “Soon I will burn him in Hell” and “What will make you realize what Hell is?” to give first characteristic, and precedes scorching skin, showing that this limbo is accompanied by active scorching.\n\nThis also follows “That is the way of Our punishment in this world, but the punishment of Hereafter is far worse” to show how far worse manifests as neither life nor death.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this is hyperbole. It is the accurate description of the overwhelming punishment for turning from remembrance and the raging Flame that summons hoarders.\n\nFor the living told to reverence, purify, shun idols, and persevere, this neither live nor die should motivate taking the Right Way while you will, reciting what is easy, establishing prayer, paying alms, lending good loan, and seeking forgiveness, so neither live nor die is not your fate, but “They will be in a life of bliss, in an elevated Garden, whose fruit hangs within reach, eat and drink joyfully” is.",
  "29": "\"scorching the skin.\" adds the second characteristic of Saqar after not letting live or die. The effect is on the body’s surface, the most sensitive.\n\n“scorching the skin” translates “lawwahatun li-l-bashar,” scorching the human skin, or appearing for humankind. Lawwahah is to scorch, to blacken, to make apparent, as with the raging Flame ripping scalps and the Fire nearly bursting. Skin, or “bashar,” is the human exterior, the same skin that was impressed by the despicable’s impressive appearance like planks leaned worthless, the same snout to be marked, and the same faces that will be gloomy and downcast covered with disgrace.\n\nScorching the skin follows neither live nor die to show that the limbo is not numb, but intensely painful on the most exposed part. It also precedes “It is overseen by nineteen keepers,” showing that scorching is administered.\n\nThis Hell is the same Hell that will summon whoever turned backs and hoarded, whose wealth did not benefit and authority was stripped.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to focus on skin graphic without link to purification. Purify your garments was commanded precisely because Hell scorches skin; purified garments in this world, through wudu and character, protect skin in the next.\n\nFor practice, protect the skin now by revering your Lord, purifying garments, and not doing favour expecting more, so the skin that would be scorched is instead among bright faces looking at their Lord, in Gardens held in honour, where no choking food, no chain, and no scorching remain, only splendid homes and excellent provision for ever and ever, obtained by the good you sent forth found with Allah better and greater.\n\nAdditional: Not letting live or die also describes the Fire that almost bursts, the raging Flame ripping scalps, and the choking food that sticks. Neither live nor die is the far worse Hereafter if only they knew, the severe punishment prepared. It is the opposite of the life of bliss in elevated Garden where they are told to eat and drink joyfully, and the excellent provision granted to those who believe and do good for ever and ever. To avoid neither live nor die, choose the reminder and take the Right Way, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so life is bliss not limbo, and provision never delays.",
  "30": "\"It is overseen by nineteen ˹keepers˺.\" gives the number of wardens after describing Saqar as not letting live or die and scorching skin. The number is precise.\n\n“It is overseen by nineteen keepers” translates “‘alayha tis‘ata ‘ashar,” over it are nineteen, as with the formidable severe angels over the Fire who never disobey, the angels on the sides of the torn sky, and the eight bearing the Throne. Nineteen is not random, as with seven heavens in layers, six Days, and fifty thousand years, all measured, destined for everything. Nineteen is appointed as test, as next verse explains, for disbelievers, People of the Book, believers, and hypocrites, to distinguish.\n\nThis follows scorching skin to show that scorching is managed, not chaotic, with wardens.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to debate nineteen as code, as some did, missing the point. The Quran says We have appointed only stern angels as wardens and made their number only as a test for disbelievers, so People of Book increase in faith, believers increase, and those with disease doubt, showing number is test, not numerology.\n\nFor the living, nineteen should increase faith, not speculation. Be among the People of the right who are not detained, who will be in Gardens asking about the wicked “What landed you in Hell? We were not of those who prayed, nor fed poor, indulged, denied the Day until the inevitable came,” and among those who take the reminder, whoever wills be mindful, as Allah wills, He is worthy to be feared and entitled to forgive, not among those for whom the number is a stumbling block that leads to the Fire’s fuel.\n\nMore: Scorching the skin, or “lawwahatun li-l-bashar,” also appears as the Fire that rips scalps, the snout marked, and the faces gloomy covered with disgrace. Scorching is the exposure of what was used to impress, the worthless planks that impress, the despicable’s withholder, and the brute’s ancient fables. Skin scorching shows that outward impressiveness without inner purification is burned. Purify your garments was commanded precisely to protect skin. To avoid scorching, be among those who guard chastity, testify honestly, and are properly observant, whose skin will be among bright faces looking at their Lord, in Gardens held in honour where no scorching remains.",
  "31": "\"We have appointed only ˹stern˺ angels as wardens of the Fire. And We have made their number only as a test for the disbelievers, so that the People of the Book will be convinced, the believers will increase in faith, those given the Scripture and the believers will have no doubts, and so those ˹hypocrites˺ with sickness in their hearts and the disbelievers will say, “What does Allah mean by this example?” In this way Allah leaves to stray whoever He wills and guides whoever He wills. And none knows the forces of your Lord except Him. And this ˹mention of Hell˺ is nothing but a reminder for humankind.\" explains the nineteen after stating it is overseen. The verse details wisdom behind the number.\n\n“We have appointed only stern angels as wardens of the Fire. And We have made their number only as a test for the disbelievers” indicates that the number is deliberate trial, as with garden test, wealth and children as test, and abundant rain as test. Test, or “fitnah,” distinguishes groups, as with the balance so people may administer justice. Disbelievers are tested by mocking the number, as with ancient fables and pure magic dismissals, while People of the Book, who know scriptural numbers, are convinced that this matches, believers increase in faith, and those with sickness and disbelievers ask “What does Allah mean by this example?”\n\n“So that People of the Book will be convinced, the believers will increase in faith, those given Scripture and believers will have no doubts” shows three positive outcomes. People of the Book, familiar with previous revelations about angels, find confirmation that the well-preserved Record is consistent, the Reminder for the whole world is not invented. Believers increase, as with the most sensible who said “Did I not urge you to say Allah willing?” and as with those who hear and obey to best ability.\n\n“and so those with sickness in their hearts and the disbelievers will say, ‘What does Allah mean by this example?’” exposes the negative reaction of hypocrites whose hearts have faltered, turned, collaborated, and whose oaths are shields, and of disbelievers who rush to mock.\n\n“In this way Allah leaves to stray whoever He wills and guides whoever He wills” shows that the same information, nineteen, guides and misguides according to disposition, as with the Light that brings from darkness for the submitted and is fuel for the deviant.\n\n“And none knows the forces of your Lord except Him” humbles, as with the unseen that none knows except Him, the appointed time that never delays, the Pen’s writing. And this mention of Hell is nothing but a reminder for humankind, as with surely this is a reminder, so let whoever wills take the Right Way, showing that even Hell’s numbers are mercy, a warning to humankind, whichever chooses to take the lead or lag behind, every soul detained except people of the right in Gardens asking about the wicked.",
  "32": "\"But no! By the moon,\" begins an oath after explaining the nineteen as reminder, returning to cosmic witnesses.\n\n“But no! By the moon” translates “kalla wa al-qamar,” No! By the moon, an oath that denies the denier’s dismissal and swears by the moon within seven heavens as a reflected light, the same moon that will be dimmed when sight is stunned and sun and moon are brought together. Moon here is the witness that the One who created seven heavens one above another, placed moon and sun, spread earth for spacious pathways, and determines day and night, is truthful about Hell.\n\nBut no, or “kalla,” is emphatic denial of the denier’s claim that “this is nothing but magic from ancients, word of a man” and of the’s sickness question “What does Allah mean by this example?” It is the same “But no!” as for ransom with everyone on earth and for submitted not equal wicked.\n\nBy the moon swears by its phases, its reflected nature, its place within the seven heavens, as with the Pen and what everyone writes, and with the positions of stars as great oath for the noble Quran.\n\nThis follows nineteen as reminder to show that the reminder is supported by moon, night, day, and mighty catastrophe.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to worship the moon, as with idols. Moon is created light, not deity, as “He is the Lord of east and west, there is no god except Him.”\n\nFor the living who are told to arise and warn, the moon’s oath should move you to take the lead, not lag, to be among the people of the right, not detained, whose Gardens are where they ask about the wicked, so the moon that will be dimmed on the Day is for you a light of reminder now that leads to Right Guidance, not to fuel for Hell.",
  "33": "\"and the night as it retreats,\" continues the oath after the moon. The night’s retreat is a daily sign.\n\n“and the night as it retreats” translates “wa al-layl idh adbara,” the night when it turns away, withdraws, as after the Day it will be like dunes and the sky split, and as with the night more impactful for recitation and the day over-occupied. Night retreats after being wrapped in clothes, after standing half the night, and as the moon wanes, showing the Lord of east and west who determines day and night, who merges night into day, who knows whether you speak secretly or openly, and who descends command between seven heavens.\n\nThis follows moon to show sequential witnesses, moon, night, day, all pointing to Hell as mighty catastrophe.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to see night retreat as mere astronomy, not as warning. Night retreat is the time of standing, reciting properly, remembering the Name wholeheartedly, and devoting, so missing it is missing Right Way.\n\nFor practice, let night retreat find you having stood, recited what is easy, established prayer, paid alms, lent good loan, so the night that retreats does not retreat from you with regret, but with light perfected, and you are among those who take the lead, not lag, behind Hell’s warning.\n\nFurther: Night as it retreats also marks the time when the wrapped who stood half the night recited properly, when worship is more impactful, and when the weighty revelation steadies. Night retreating signals dawn’s arrival for the over-occupied day to seek bounty and remember often, for the garden owners to have said “Allah willing,” and for the disbelievers to rush headlong to mock. Retreat is mercy, as with the way out for the mindful, provision from sources never imagined, and ease after hardship. Let night retreat find you having taken the lead for the warning to humankind, so you are among the people of the right in Gardens asking about the wicked, not among the spooked zebras fleeing from a lion, nor those wishing for a personal letter.",
  "34": "\"and the day as it breaks!\" completes the triple oath after the moon and the night as it retreats. The breaking day is a witness.\n\n“and the day as it breaks!” translates “wa al-subh idha asfara,” the morning when it shines, brightens. Breaking day is the light that follows night’s retreat, as after the wrapped stands half the night and recites properly, and after the over-occupied day begins. It is the day that delights planters with rain then withers to chaff, the day the garden owners went early fixated, the day the caravan fanfare drew crowds, and the Day every soul will be detained except the people of the right.\n\nThis oath follows moon and night to show the full cycle, moon, night, day, that testifies Hell is mightiest catastrophe, a warning to humankind, to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to worship daybreak. Daybreak is created, like the moon’s reflected light and sun’s radiant lamp, whose Lord is east and west, no god except Him, take Him as Trustee.\n\nFor the covered who arises and warns, the breaking day signals that the night’s impact should now be lived. Let the day break find you not among spooked zebras fleeing from a lion from the reminder, but among those who take the lead for the warning, whose Gardens are where they ask about the wicked, so breaking day is for you a miser’s dawn that reveals you were among the mindful, not the indulging.\n\nExtra: Day as it breaks also completes the moon and night retreat sequence, showing the full day-night cycle that the Lord of east and west determines, who merges night into day and knows what is hidden in hearts. Breaking day brings the garden owners’ early harvest, the caravan’s fanfare, and the indulging and amusing that lasts until they face their Day. For the wrapped who stood at night, day breaks as opportunity to be mindful to best ability, hear and obey, spend, and be saved from selfishness, so breaking day is not the Trumpet’s difficult Day but the day you take the lead, among the people of the right whose Gardens are held in honour.",
  "35": "\"Surely Hell is one of the mightiest catastrophes—\" declares Hell’s magnitude after swearing by moon, night, and day. The statement quantifies warning.\n\n“Surely Hell is one of the mightiest catastrophes—” translates “innaha la-ihda al-kubar,” it is one of the greatest, most immense calamities. Kubar is great, enormous, as with seven heavens one above another, the Blessed in Whose Hands rests all authority, Most Capable, and the weighty revelation. Hell as mightiest catastrophe parallels the Fire that almost bursts, the raging Flame ripping scalps, the Fire that does not let live or die and scorches skin overseen by nineteen.\n\nMightiest indicates Hell is not a minor deterrent, but among the greatest, alongside the Inevitable Hour and Striking Disaster, the Trumpet’s one blast crushing earth and mountains, the sky torn frail.\n\nThis follows oaths to give weight to the warning, and precedes “a warning to humankind, to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag,” showing purpose.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think mightiest catastrophe invites despair that forgets that Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, who has accepted repentance, who multiplies good loans, and who gives Gardens.\n\nFor the wrapped who stands and recites, Hell as mightiest catastrophe is the reason to arise and warn, revere, purify, shun idols, not expect more, and persevere. For you who hear, let mightiest catastrophe move you to take the Right Way now, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so mightiest catastrophe is for you a warning taken, not a catastrophe entered, and your catastrophe is instead the better garden hoped for.\n\nMore: Mightiest catastrophes also includes the Inevitable Hour, Striking Disaster, the Trumpet’s one blast crushing earth and mountains, sky torn frail, angels on sides, Throne borne by eight, and presentation with no secrets hidden. Hell as mightiest stands among them, like Laza ripping scalps, Jahim, and Saqar that does not let live. Mightiest is not to terrify into inaction like Jonah in distress, but to motivate beautiful patience, night worship more impactful, and proper observance, so mightiest is met with Gardens held in honour where you are not among the spooked zebras fleeing but among the mindful who chose to take the lead.",
  "36": "\"a warning to humankind,\" defines Hell’s function after calling it mightiest catastrophe. The warning is universal.\n\n“a warning to humankind,” translates “nadhiran li-l-bashar,” a warner for humanity. Warning is the same as the Messenger’s warn all, Noah’s warn before painful punishment, the keepers asking “Did a warner not come?” and the reminder to the whole world, the Ark for attentive ears, and the Pen. Humankind, or “bashar,” includes the despicable withholder, the garden owners, the luxury deniers, the wicked who will be asked what landed you in Hell, and the people of the right who will ask.\n\nThis follows mightiest catastrophe to show that might is for warning, not for delight in suffering.\n\nA warning to humankind indicates that Hell’s mention is mercy, like shackles, raging Fire, choking food, dunes that warn before they become reality, and like the garden’s ashes that warn markets.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear warning to humankind as only for the disbelievers. It is for humankind, including the believers told to take the lead.\n\nFor the covered who arises, warning to humankind is the content, Hell’s might, delivered with outstanding character, never-ending reward, and beautiful patience, so humankind, whichever chooses to take the lead or lag, has clear choice, and your choice can be the lead, among the people of the right, not detained, asking about the wicked, whose Gardens are held in honour.\n\nFurther: Warning to humankind also recalls the keepers asking “Did a warner not come?” on each cast, the wall with gate between grace and torment, and the Ark as reminder for attentive ears. Warning is the Pen and what everyone writes, the Reminder that would humble a mountain, the Light that brings from darkness. Humankind here includes the luxury deniers, the despicable, the garden fixated, and the excepted who pray. For the covered who arises and warns, warning to humankind is your task, to convey the truth from Allah and deliver His messages, so humankind, whichever chooses to take the lead or lag, has clear choice, and your warning becomes the better garden hoped for after turning.",
  "37": "\"to whichever of you chooses to take the lead or lag behind.\" clarifies who the warning is for after calling Hell a warning to humankind. The choice is individual.\n\n“to whichever of you chooses to take the lead or lag behind” translates “li-man sha’a minkum an yataqaddama aw yata’akhkhar,” for whoever among you wills to go forward or remain behind. Take the lead, or “yataqaddama,” is to advance, as with competing for forgiveness and Paradise as vast as heavens and earth, taking the Right Way, and the helpers who took the lead. Lag behind, or “yata’akhkhar,” is to stay, as with persisting in denial and acting arrogantly, pressing fingers and covering, and indulging and amusing until they face their Day.\n\nWhichever of you indicates personal responsibility, as with every soul detained for what it has done, and you will be presented with no secret hidden, and the Day they come forth racing swiftly.\n\nThis follows warning to humankind to show that warning respects will, as with “Surely this is a reminder. So let whoever wills take the Right Way,” but they cannot unless Allah wills, He is worthy to be feared and entitled to forgive.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think lead or lag are neutral. Lead is toward the Messenger reciting, purifying, teaching, and Gardens; lag is toward Saqar, the nineteen, and the choking food.\n\nFor practice, choose to take the lead now. Recite what is easy, stand half the night or less or more, remember the Name wholeheartedly, take Trustee, be patient and depart courteously, leave luxury deniers for a little while, so the choice to take the lead is recorded by the pen, and you are among those in Gardens held in honour, not among spooked zebras fleeing from the reminder.\n\nAdditional: To whichever chooses to take the lead or lag also parallels “Let whoever wills take the Right Way to your Lord” and “Surely this is a reminder, so let whoever wills be mindful.” Lead is to advance as the helpers who loved emigrants and the disciples who stood with Jesus, lag is to remain as the hypocrites who thought every cry against them and the luxury deniers who indulged. Choice is real, as with the test of best deeds, and Allah guides whoever He wills and leaves to stray whoever He wills after the reminder’s number test. Choose to take the lead now, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so lead is your Gardens where you ask about the wicked, not the wicked’s regret.",
  "38": "\"Every soul will be detained for what it has done,\" states the principle after choice to lead or lag. The verse defines detention.\n\n“Every soul will be detained for what it has done” translates “kullu nafsin bi-ma kasabat rahinah,” every soul is held in pledge for what it earned. Rahinah is detained, pledged, as with trusts and covenants the faithful are true to, and as with the good sent forth found with Allah better and greater. Every soul, or “kullu nafs,” includes the one created alone with wealth and children, the garden owners, the luxury deniers, the despicable, and the excepted who pray consistently, give rightful share, and guard chastity.\n\nDetained for what it has done indicates that deeds pledge the soul, like a debtor held until payment, as with the garden reduced to ashes while they slept, the severe account for rebellious societies, and the Fire that does not let live.\n\nThis follows lead or lag to show that leading or lagging pledges the soul accordingly, and precedes except the people of the right, who are not detained, showing an exception, a release from pledge, like freeing a slave before touching.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think detention is arbitrary. Detention is for what it has done, not for lineage, honour that expels inferior, or wealth that diverts.\n\nFor the detained, the way out is what the next verses show, sincere repentance that perfects light, being among people of the right, and the good loan multiplied.\n\nFor the living, live as one who will be detained, so your doing is the exception’s doing, praying, feeding the poor, not indulging, not denying the Day until the inevitable came, so detention is released and you are in Gardens asking about the wicked, not detained.\n\nMore: Every soul detained also echoes “He will inform you of what you have done and that is easy” and “We have ordained death and We cannot be prevented from transforming.” Detention is pledge, like trusts and covenants that the faithful guard, and like the good sent forth found with Allah better and greater. Every soul includes the one created alone with wealth and children, the garden owners, and the properly observant whose Gardens are held in honour but who are released. To be detained is to be held until the good you sent forth is weighed, like the provision from sources never imagined and the ease after hardship. Live as one whose pledge is light, not heavy with withholding good and calling ancient fables.",
  "39": "\"except the people of the right,\" gives the exception to every soul detained. The people of the right are released from pledge.\n\n“except the people of the right” translates “illa ashab al-yamin,” save the companions of the right, the people of the right hand who will cry happily “Read my record, I surely knew I would face my reckoning,” whose life is bliss in elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach and they are told to eat and drink joyfully, who are the submitted not wicked, whose Gardens of Bliss with their Lord are distinguished.\n\nPeople of the right are the same as the excepted from halu‘ impatience, those who pray consistently, give rightful share to the asker and deprived, firmly believe in the Day, fear punishment knowing none should feel secure, guard chastity, are true to trusts, honest in testimony, and properly observant, whose Gardens are held in honour. They are also those who were not of those who prayed? No, they are the opposite, who prayed, fed poor, did not indulge, and did not deny.\n\nException indicates that pledge is not equal. The submitted are not treated like the wicked, how do you judge, and residents of Fire cannot be equal to residents of Paradise, only the latter are truly successful.\n\nThis follows every soul detained to show that detention is not universal fate, and precedes who will be in Gardens asking about the wicked, showing where the excepted are.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to claim people of the right by label without deeds. Exception is for those whose doing matches, as with the garden owners after turning and hoping for better.\n\nFor practice, be among people of the right now, take the Right Way to your Lord, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so the soul detained for what it has done is released, and you are among the Gardens asking, not among the detained fueled for Hell like the deviant.",
  "40": "\"who will be in Gardens, asking one another\" describes where the excepted people of the right will be and what they will do. The scene is serene.\n\n“who will be in Gardens, asking one another” translates the excepted in Gardens, or “jannat,” plural, as with Gardens under rivers for ever and ever, excellent provision, splendid homes, and Gardens of Bliss, held in honour, elevated. Asking one another indicates conversation, mutual inquiry, as with the helpers and emigrants’ love and the later supplicants who pray for forgiveness without bitterness, the opposite of turning on each other throwing blame after garden ashes and the Fire’s residents confessing then being told away.\n\nIn Gardens, the people of the right ask about the wicked, showing curiosity and gratitude, not gloating, as with the wall with a gate where grace and torment are separated but dialogue across occurs, like the tormented crying “Were we not with you?” and being answered “Yes but you were tempted...”\n\nThis follows except the people of the right to show that not being detained means being in Gardens asking.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear asking as idle curiosity. Asking about the wicked is to understand cause, as next verses list, “What has landed you in Hell? We were not of those who prayed, nor fed poor, indulged, denied until the inevitable came.”\n\nFor the living, be among those in Gardens asking, by being among those wrapped who stood, recited properly, remembered wholeheartedly, took Trustee, were patient, left deniers, and persevered, so your Gardens conversation is with fellow honoured, not with keepers asking “Did a warner not come?” while you are being cast.\n\nFurther: Who will be in Gardens, asking one another also reflects the wall where tormented cry “Were we not with you?” and are answered “Yes but you were tempted,” and the keepers’ question. Gardens asking is the gracious counterpart, where the people of the right, not detained, enjoy leisure to inquire with gratitude, as with the helpers’ love and later supplicants’ prayer without bitterness. Asking one another shows community, not isolation like no close friend on the Day the sky is molten brass. To be among askers, be among the wrapped who stood half the night, remembered wholeheartedly, took Trustee, and left luxury deniers for a little while, so asking in Gardens is your bliss, not being asked about as wicked.",
  "41": "\"about the wicked ˹who will then be asked˺:\" specifies the subject of the Gardens’ asking. The wicked are the detained, not excepted.\n\n“about the wicked who will then be asked:” translates “‘an al-mujrimin,” concerning the criminals, the wicked, the same as the submitted not treated like wicked, and as with the wicked who will wish to ransom with everyone on earth, the wicked summoned by Laza, and the deviant fuel for Hell. Wicked here are those detained for what they did, residents of the Blaze who will have no close friend, whose food is pus.\n\nWho will then be asked indicates the Gardens’ residents will inquire and the wicked will be asked what landed you in Hell, as next verses record the wicked’s answer listing not praying, not feeding poor, indulging, denying until inevitable.\n\nThis follows who will be in Gardens asking one another to show that Gardens’ bliss includes understanding justice, not ignorance, as with attentive ears that grasp the Ark reminder and the pen that writes.\n\nWicked who will then be asked are not the garden owners after they turned, but those who persisted, like Pharaoh disobeying and being seized sternly, Thamud’s blast, ‘Ad’s wind, and the one left to Me with wealth and children who said magic and word of a man and will be burned in Hell that does not let live.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use wicked as slur for anyone who errs. Wicked here are those detained for habitual doing, not for occasional lapse forgiven by the Most Appreciative, Most Forbearing.\n\nFor practice, ask now while in the world what lands in Hell, learn from the wicked’s own answer, and avoid it, being among those who pray, feed poor, do not indulge along with others, and do not deny the Day, so you are the asker in Gardens, not the asked wicked in Hell.",
  "42": "\"“What has landed you in Hell?”\" quotes the Gardens’ question to the wicked. The inquiry seeks cause, not mockery.\n\n“‘What has landed you in Hell?’” translates “ma salakakum fi saqar,” what put you into Saqar? Salaka is to make enter, to cause to enter, as with “We will burn him in Hell” and “They will be in Gardens, asking one another about the wicked.” Saqar is the same Hell that does not let live or die, scorching skin, overseen by nineteen, the raging Flame ripping scalps, the choking food and painful punishment in store. The question is asked by the people of the right to the wicked, parallel to the keepers asking “Did a warner not come?” and the wall’s dialogue where tormented cry “Were we not with you?”\n\nThe question expects listing, as the wicked reply with four causes, not praying, not feeding poor, indulging, denying until inevitable came.\n\nThis follows about the wicked who will then be asked, beginning the dialogue.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as gloating. The Gardens’ asking is to learn and to be grateful, as with the helpers’ love and the later supplicants’ forgiveness without bitterness.\n\nFor the living, ask yourself now, what would land me in Saqar, and answer with the wicked’s own list, then correct it. Pray, feed poor, do not indulge along with others, and do not deny the Day, so the answer on that Day is not your answer, but the answer of those held in honour who are told to eat and drink joyfully for what they did in days gone by.\n\nExtra: “What has landed you in Hell?” also inverts the garden owners’ “What has landed you in?” but for Saqar, which does not let live. The Gardens’ residents ask not to gloat, but to confirm that every soul detained for what it has done is just, as with the balance so people may administer justice and the pen writing. The wicked’s four answers—not praying, not feeding poor, indulging with waders, denying until inevitable—teach what lands, so the living can avoid. For you who hear now, let what landed them warn you to land in Gardens, not Hell, by being among those who pray, feed poor, do not wade, and believe in the Day until the inevitable comes as Gardens, not Saqar.",
  "43": "\"They will reply, “We were not of those who prayed,\"\" begins the wicked’s answer to what landed you in Saqar. The first cause is abandoning prayer.\n\n“They will reply, ‘We were not of those who prayed,’” translates the wicked’s confession, as with “If only we had listened and reasoned we would not be among residents of Blaze,” and “We have been deprived” after garden ashes. We were not of those who prayed indicates they were among those who were distressed when touched with evil and withholding when touched with good, except those who pray, consistently performing, properly observant, whose Gardens are held in honour. Not praying is the root, as with the despicable and garden owners who omitted “Allah willing” and the pagans who rushed to mock instead of proceeding diligently to remembrance on Friday.\n\nPrayed here includes the Messenger’s standing for nearly two-thirds, half, or a third, properly reciting, remembering the Name wholeheartedly, and the Friday call.\n\nThis follows what has landed you in Hell to give first reason, and precedes nor did we feed the poor, showing that prayer and feeding are paired, as with consistent prayer and rightful share for the asker and deprived.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to reduce not praying to missing a single prayer. Not of those who prayed indicates habitual abandonment, as with persisting in disbelief and turning away.\n\nFor practice, be of those who prayed, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, and lend good loan, so your reply on that Day is not “We were not of those who prayed,” but “Read my record, I surely knew I would face my reckoning,” and your life is bliss in elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach.\n\nMore: “We were not of those who prayed” also contrasts with the Messenger standing for nearly two-thirds, half, or a third, and the excepted who consistently perform and properly observe. Not of those who prayed is not a single missed prayer but a habit of being among those who crawl facedown vs walk upright, who rush to mock from right and left, who expect Garden without traits. To be of those who prayed, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so your reply is not this, but “Read my record, I knew I would face my reckoning,” and your life is bliss in elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach.",
  "44": "\"nor did we feed the poor.\" gives the second cause for entering Saqar, after not praying. The pair covers worship of Creator and mercy to creation.\n\n“nor did we feed the poor” translates “wa lam naku nut‘imu al-miskin,” we did not feed the needy. Feeding the poor is the rightful share for the asker and deprived, the good loan, the “let the poor enter your garden today,” and the opposite of “Do not spend on those with the Messenger so they break away” and “Do not let any poor enter today.” Not feeding includes not encouraging feeding, as with the withholder of good, transgressor, evildoer, brute.\n\nThis follows not praying to show that prayer without feeding is incomplete, as with the garden owners who prayed not and excluded poor and found ashes, and the hypocrites who said “we bear witness” but hindered.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think feeding poor is merely optional charity. Here it is among the four that land in Saqar, alongside not praying, indulging, and denying, showing it is central.\n\nFor the living who are told to give rightful share, feed the poor now, let poor enter, give good loans, and be true to trusts, so the answer “nor did we feed the poor” is not yours, and the Gardens where you are told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by, where the poor are now companions, becomes your answer, and the choking food in store for luxury deniers is not yours.\n\nAdditional: Nor did we feed the poor also mirrors the rightful share for the asker and deprived, the “let the poor enter your garden today” that garden owners violated, and the “do not spend on those with the Messenger” that hypocrites urged. Not feeding is the practical expression of “which powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate? They are lost in delusion” and “who will provide if He withholds? They persist in arrogance.” Feeding the poor now involves known right, courteous nursing compensation, and provision according to means, all of which make wealth a trust, not a shield. To avoid this answer, feed, encourage feeding, and be true to trusts, so you are told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by, not choked by food that sticks.",
  "45": "\"We used to indulge ˹in falsehood˺ along with others,\" gives the third cause for Saqar. The indulgence was social.\n\n“We used to indulge in falsehood along with others” translates “wa kunna nakhudu ma‘a al-kha’idin,” we used to wade into vain talk with those who wade, as with “let them indulge in falsehood and amuse themselves until they face their Day” and “we used to indulge along with others” is the same as indulging and amusing, play, amusement, luxury, boasting, competition that becomes chaff, and as with rushing headlong in groups to mock from right and left. Wading, or “khawd,” is to plunge into falsehood, to wade, as with indulging along with others until inevitable came, and as with those who take the reminder as Zabar, spooked zebras fleeing.\n\nThis follows not feeding the poor to show that social indulgence accompanies neglect, and precedes denying the Day until inevitable came, showing progression from neglect to indulgence to denial.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to see indulging as harmless banter. Indulging here is the despicable’s slander, gossip-monger, and the caravan fanfare that drew crowds from the pulpit, all wading that grieves believers.\n\nFor practice, do not wade with waders. Instead, make room when told, make private talk for goodness and righteousness, depart courteously from deniers, and be among those who pray consistently, whose Gardens are held in honour, so wading is replaced by reciting properly in a measured way at night, and the inevitable that came to the wicked does not come to you as Saqar but as Gardens where you ask about the wicked with gratitude, not as one of them.\n\nFurther: We used to indulge along with others also describes indulgence that the verse “let them indulge and amuse until they face their Day” permits temporarily for the luxury deniers left to Allah. Indulging with waders is wading in falsehood, play, luxury, boasting, competition that becomes chaff, and rushing to mock, all delusion that submitted and wicked should not be equated. Indulging is the opposite of being wrapped who stood at night reciting properly, more impactful, suitable for recitation. To not indulge, make private talk for goodness and righteousness, make room when told, and remember often after dispersing to seek bounty, so wading is replaced by walking upright and indulging is replaced by honour in Gardens held in honour.",
  "46": "\"and deny the Day of Judgment,\" gives the fourth cause for Saqar after indulging. Denial is the doctrinal root.\n\n“and deny the Day of Judgment” translates “wa kunna nukadhdhibu bi-yawm al-din,” we used to deny the Day of recompense, the mutual loss and gain, the Inevitable, the Striking, the Day fifty thousand years they see as far, the Day they will be presented with no secret hidden, when the Trumpet is sounded as difficult Day, and the Day they come forth racing to an idol with eyes downcast. Denying the Day underlies not praying, not feeding, and wading, as with those who felt secure that One in heaven will not swallow or unleash stones, who thought strongholds put them out of reach, and who said ancient fables and pure magic.\n\nThis follows indulging to show that indulgence fosters denial, and precedes until the inevitable came to us, showing denial persisted until death.\n\nDenying the Day mirrors the challenger demanding punishment bound to come and the disbelievers who see it as far but We see it as near, and the garden owners who left no thought for Allah’s Will.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit denial to verbal claim. Denial includes practical denial, living as if smoothed earth is not for resurrection to Him, as with the one who crawls facedown vs walks upright.\n\nFor the living, do not deny the Day. Firmly believe in the Day of Judgment as the excepted do, fear punishment knowing none should feel secure, and walk upright on the Straight Path, so denial is replaced by certainty that I surely knew I would face my reckoning, and the Day that is difficult far from easy for disbelievers becomes for you the Day where light shines ahead and you are told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by.",
  "47": "\"until the inevitable came to us.”\" completes the wicked’s four causes, marking the end point of persistence. The inevitable is death that brings certainty.\n\n“until the inevitable came to us” translates “hatta atana al-yaqin,” until certainty came to us, meaning death, the appointed time that can never be delayed, the soul reaching the throat while you are looking on, nearer than you but you cannot see, then asked to bring that soul back if not subject, the Trumpet, the Waqi‘ah that will have come to pass. Yaqin here is death, the certainty that ends indulging, as with the garden reduced to ashes while they slept and the royal denier’s fate unbearable.\n\nThis follows deny the Day to show that denial lasted until death made denial impossible, as with the keepers asking “Did a warner not come?” and they reply “Yes, but we denied,” and the residents confessing sins then being told away.\n\nUntil indicates persistence, as with they persisted in disobeying, devised tremendous plot, urged idols, led many astray, and found no helper against Allah, then admitted into Fire.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think inevitable is the Inevitable Hour alone. It is personal certainty, death, which brings the Hour near, as with “if we return to Medina, the honourable will definitely expel the inferior, but honour belongs to Allah” and soon We will mark his snout.\n\nFor the living, do not wait until the inevitable comes to you. Take the Right Way to your Lord now, while this is a reminder, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so the inevitable that comes is not Saqar that does not let live or die, but the Gardens where the inevitable is bliss, and you are among the people of the right, not the four-cause wicked who persisted until certainty ended indulgence.",
  "48": "\"So the pleas of intercessors will be of no benefit to them.\" states the consequence after the wicked’s four causes and until the inevitable came. Intercession is cut.\n\n“So the pleas of intercessors will be of no benefit to them” translates “fa-ma tanfa‘uhum shafa‘atu al-shafi‘in,” intercession of intercessors will not benefit them. Intercessors, or “shafi‘in,” are those who plead, as with the mesmerizing “Come, the Messenger will pray for you to be forgiven” they turned heads in disgust from, and “It is the same whether you pray for forgiveness or not, Allah will not forgive them for their hearts are sealed,” and the Fire’s angels who never disobey, who do not intercede against command.\n\nThis follows until the inevitable came to show that after persistence in not praying, not feeding, indulging, denying until death, intercession is too late, as with every soul detained for what it has done and the pleas that would be “Read my record” for the right.\n\nPleas of intercessors includes human and angelic pleading, but not for the wicked who are fuel for Hell, as with those who will have no close friend here and whose food is pus.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to deny intercession altogether. Intercession benefits the mindful who are taught, those who take the Right Way, whose light shines, as with the Messenger’s prayer for believers and the Gardens’ intercession, but not for those who persisted until certainty.\n\nFor the living, seek intercession now by being among those who pray, feed poor, do not indulge, and believe in the Day, so intercession will benefit you, as with “My Lord! Forgive me, my parents, and whoever enters my house in faith, and all believing men and women,” and increase not wrongdoers except in loss, where the former are among the excepted, not the four-cause wicked.",
  "49": "\"Now, what is the matter with them that they are turning away from the reminder,\" asks about the living deniers after the wicked’s confession and the cut intercession. The reminder is the same that would humble a mountain.\n\n“Now, what is the matter with them that they are turning away from the reminder” translates “fa-ma lahum ‘an al-tadhkirati mu‘ridin,” what is with them turning from the Reminder, the Tadhkirah that the Quran is, the Reminder to the whole world, the reminder for attentive ears, the reminder that surely this is a reminder, so let whoever wills take the Right Way, and this Hell mention is nothing but a reminder for humankind. Turning away, or “mu‘ridin,” is to avert, as with pressing fingers, covering clothes, persisting, acting arrogantly, rushing headlong, and indulging.\n\nThis follows intercession will not benefit the four-cause wicked to show that the living still have a chance, but they are turning away as spooked zebras next.\n\nWhat is the matter with them that they are turning away echoes “What is the matter with you? How do you judge?” and “Should We treat submitted like wicked?”\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think turning away is passive. It is active, like spooked zebras fleeing a lion.\n\nFor practice, do not turn away from the reminder. Be among those wrapped who stand half the night and recite properly, who remember wholeheartedly, take Trustee, be patient, leave luxury deniers for a little while, so shackles, raging Fire, choking food, and dunes are not your reminder as Hell, but the Gardens where you ask about the wicked with gratitude are yours.\n\nExtra: Turning away from the reminder also describes the earlier pressing fingers into ears, covering with clothes, persisting, acting arrogantly, re-contemplating in frustration, frowning, scowling, turning his back and acting arrogantly, saying magic and word of a man. Turning away is not a single glance, but a habit, as with feeling secure that One in heaven will not swallow or unleash stones. The reminder that would humble a mountain, that is weighty soon sent, that is Light, that is Pen, is turned from, so the turners become spooked zebras. To not turn, be among those in awe without seeing whose mighty reward is forgiveness, whose attentive ears grasp the Ark, and who take the Right Way while you will, so turning is toward the Trustee of east and west, not away.",
  "50": "\"as if they were spooked zebras\" compares the turners from the reminder to frightened animals. The simile captures panic.\n\n“as if they were spooked zebras” translates “ka-annahum humurun mustanfirah,” like donkeys, zebras, startled, wild asses. Humur are wild donkeys, zebras, known for shying, bolting when startled. Mustanfirah is to be frightened, fleeing, as with indulging and amusing until they face their Day and then fleeing from a lion.\n\nThis follows turning away from the reminder to illustrate how turning looks, and precedes fleeing from a lion to complete the image.\n\nSpooked zebras flee instinctively from a predator, but these flee from the reminder that is mercy, that showers rain, supplies wealth, gives gardens, that would humble a mountain, and that is weighty revelation. Their flight is perverse, like the despicable who withholds good and the garden owners who exclude poor, fleeing from what would benefit.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to mock the simile as insult to animals. It is to show behavior, as with donkey carrying books who was entrusted with Torah but failed.\n\nFor the living, do not be spooked zebras. Be among those in awe without seeing, attentive ears that grasp, who listen and reason so as not to be among residents of Blaze who lament if only we had listened, and among those who walk upright, not crawl facedown, so the reminder that is mightiest catastrophe as warning becomes your Gardens of Bliss with your Lord, not the lion you flee.\n\nMore: Spooked zebras also illustrate the despicable’s slanderer, gossip-monger, withholder, transgressor, brute who flee from truth, and the garden owners who were spooked after seeing devastation and said “We lost our way, we are deprived.” Zebras flee by instinct from a lion, but humans fleeing from the reminder flee from mercy, from the shower of abundant rain, gardens and rivers promised to those who seek forgiveness, and from the Gardens of Bliss with Hour Lord. Fleeing makes them prey to the raging Flame that rips scalps. To not be spooked, be calm, listen and reason, so you are not among the spooked but among the patient who endure denial with beautiful patience, not like Jonah, and whose Gardens are held in honour where no lion is feared.",
  "51": "\"fleeing from a lion?\" completes the simile for the reminder turners. The lion makes the panic specific.\n\n“fleeing from a lion?” translates “farrat min qaswarah,” fleeing from a lion, a powerful predator. Qaswarah is lion, as with the raging Flame that rips scalps, the Blaze that almost bursts, and the Fire that does not let live. Fleeing from a lion is rational for zebras, but fleeing from the reminder is irrational, like fleeing from the rain that would shower abundantly if they sought forgiveness, the gardens and rivers supplied, and the Gardens of Bliss promised.\n\nThis follows spooked zebras to finish the image, and precedes “In fact, each one of them wishes to be given a personal letter for all to read,” showing that fleeing from the reminder is paired with wishing for personal entitlement.\n\nFleeing from a lion echoes the despicable’s slander and the garden owners’ fixated exclusion, now as flight.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think the reminder is the lion. The reminder is not predator, but those turning treat it as if it were, because their hearts are sealed after believing then abandoning, and they think every cry is against them.\n\nFor practice, do not flee from the reminder as from a lion. Flee to it, as you flee to the Trustee of east and west, as you devote yourself wholeheartedly, as you take the Right Way, so the lion you would have fled becomes the Gardens where you are safe, and the reminder that was a warning to humankind to take the lead becomes your lead, not your lag, and you are not spooked zebras but among the people of the right, not detained.\n\nFurther: Fleeing from a lion also contrasts with the lion-like Hell that is mightiest catastrophe, warning to humankind. Fleeing from the reminder as from a lion is ironic, because the reminder warns of the lion-like Hell, so fleeing from warning flees toward the lion. The true lion to flee is Hell that does not let live, scorching skin, overseen by nineteen, so fleeing from the reminder is fleeing toward that lion. To flee correctly, flee from Hell to the reminder, as you flee to the Trustee of east and west, as you devote yourself wholeheartedly, and as you persevere for your Lord, so fleeing becomes taking the lead for the warning, not lagging behind it, and you are among the people of the right.",
  "52": "\"In fact, each one of them wishes to be given a ˹personal˺ letter ˹from Allah˺ for all ˹to read˺.\" exposes the wish behind fleeing from the reminder. The turners want personal entitlement.\n\n“In fact, each one of them wishes to be given a personal letter from Allah for all to read” translates “bal yuridu kullu imri’in minhum an yu’ta suhufan munashsharah,” each person among them wants to be given spread scriptures, open pages. Suhuf, pages, like the well-preserved Record only purified touch, the Pen and what everyone writes, and the book in which you read that you will have whatever you choose. Munashsharah, spread, published, for all to read, indicates they want a personal, public letter from Allah addressed to them individually, as with the garden owners who wanted all fruit, the challenger demanding punishment, and the despicable expecting honour will expel inferior.\n\nEach one of them indicates personal desire, not collective, similar to “Does every one of them expect to be admitted into a Garden of Bliss?”\n\nThis follows fleeing from a lion to show contradiction, fleeing from universal reminder while demanding private letters.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think personal revelation is the norm. Revelation is the Reminder for the whole world, the weighty revelation soon sent, the Light, the Balance, delivered via chosen messengers with angel-guards, not via personal letters to each.\n\nFor the living, abandon the wish for a personal letter and accept the spread pages already given, the Quran that would humble a mountain, the Reminder that surely this is a reminder, so let whoever wills take the Right Way, recite what is easy, and be among those who are mindful, whose light shines ahead, whose Gardens are held in honour, not among spooked zebras who flee the shared pages while wishing for private ones.\n\nThis personal-letter wish also parallels the caravan fanfare that drew crowds from the pulpit on Friday and the garden owners’ wish to harvest all early. Personal letters sought as guarantee are the opposite of the shared Reminder that is enough for the mindful. The Garden’s excellent provision and the raging Flame’s choking food are both distributed according to the shared pages, not private letters, so the wish remains the delusion of those who do not fear the Hereafter and who flee from the reminder as spooked zebras.",
  "53": "\"But no! In fact, they do not fear the Hereafter.\" refutes the wish for a personal letter and gives the true cause for turning, fleeing, and wishing.\n\n“But no! In fact, they do not fear the Hereafter” translates “kalla bal la yakhafuna al-akhirah,” No! Rather they do not fear the Hereafter. Kalla, emphatic no, as with “But no! By the moon, and the night as it retreats, and the day as it breaks, Hell is one of the mightiest catastrophes, a warning to humankind.” Not fearing the Hereafter is the root, as with feeling secure that One in heaven will not swallow or unleash stones, seeing the Day as far, not near, and presuming wealth and children divert without loss.\n\nNot fearing the Hereafter explains not praying, not feeding poor, indulging, denying until inevitable came, turning from reminder as spooked zebras, and wishing for personal letters. If Hereafter were feared, they would be among those in awe without seeing who have forgiveness and mighty reward, who firmly believe in the Day, fear punishment knowing none should feel secure, and guard chastity, true to trusts, honest in testimony, properly observant.\n\nThis follows each one wishes for a personal letter to show that wish is not about evidence, but about not fearing.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear “do not fear” as simple lack of emotion. Not fearing is practical, leading to indulging and amusing until they face their Day, and to leaving the True losers diverted by wealth and children.\n\nFor practice, fear the Hereafter now, not with despair that denies the Most Appreciative, Most Forbearing who multiplies good loans, but with awe that makes you take the lead for the warning, be among people of the right in Gardens asking about wicked, and be among the mindful who take the Right Way while you will, reciting what is easy, establishing prayer, paying alms, lending good loan, and seeking forgiveness, so fear becomes your Gardens held in honour, not Hell’s mightiest catastrophe.",
  "54": "\"Enough! Surely this ˹Quran˺ is a reminder.\" declares sufficiency after describing turning from the reminder as spooked zebras and wishing for personal letters. The exclamation stops debate.\n\n“Enough! Surely this Quran is a reminder” translates “kalla innahu tadhkirah,” No! Indeed it is a reminder. Enough, or “kalla,” is the emphatic no that denies their wish for personal letters and their fleeing, as with “But no! By the moon... Hell is mightiest catastrophe.” Surely this Quran is a reminder indicates that the reminder already given, the weighty revelation soon sent, the Light that brings from darkness, the Pen and what everyone writes, the Ark for attentive ears, is sufficient. Reminder, or “tadhkirah,” is what would humble a mountain, what the wrapped is told to stand and recite properly, and what the covered is told to arise and warn with.\n\nThis follows “they do not fear the Hereafter” to show that remedy is not personal letters but fearing the Hereafter through the reminder at hand.\n\nA reminder’s sufficiency means personal letters are unnecessary; the shared Reminder is personal enough for each who wills.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat “Enough!” as dismissal of further guidance. It is dismissal of their demand for special treatment.\n\nFor the living, Enough should end your wish for a private miracle. The Reminder is here, recited what is easy, established prayer, alms, good loan, and forgiveness sought. That is enough to move from spooked zebra to the people of the right in Gardens asking about the wicked, so enough becomes your sufficiency for honour, not your regret when you say, “We were not of those who prayed.”\n\nAdditional: Enough also echoes “But no! There will certainly be a raging Flame” and “But no! Indeed, they already know what We created them from,” where “kalla” denies false expectation. Enough here denies the need for personal letters and the flight like zebras. The Reminder is enough because it is the same Reminder that would humble a mountain, that is weighty soon sent, that is guarded by nineteen, and that is a warning to humankind to take the lead. Enough for the wrapped who stands at night becomes the light that perfects, the Gardens held in honour, and the never-ending reward, so enough is not minimal but sufficient for honour.",
  "55": "\"So let whoever wills be mindful of it.\" invites volition after declaring the Quran is a reminder. The choice is yours.\n\n“So let whoever wills be mindful of it” translates “fa-man sha’a dhakarah,” whoever wills may remember it, be mindful. Will, or “sha’a,” is the same will as “to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag” after Hell as warning, and “Surely this is a reminder. So let whoever wills take the Right Way.” Mindful, or “dhakara,” is to remember, to be humbled at remembrance, to listen and reason so you are not among residents of Blaze who lament if only we had listened, and to be among those in awe without seeing whose mighty reward is forgiveness.\n\nWhoever wills indicates responsibility, as with every soul detained for what it has done, and you will be presented with no secret hidden. It also connects to “But they cannot do so unless Allah wills. He alone is worthy to be feared and entitled to forgive,” showing that willing is enabled by divine will, yet the invitation is genuine.\n\nThis follows Enough! Surely this Quran is a reminder to show what is to be mindful of.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read “whoever wills” as implying you can be mindful without divine help, like the powerless force that would help instead of the Most Compassionate. Willing needs the One who smooths earth for spacious pathways and descends command between seven heavens.\n\nFor practice, will to be mindful now. Remember the Name wholeheartedly, devote, take Trustee, stand half the night or less or more, recite properly, so willing becomes the lead that is Gardens held in honour, not the lag that is detained, and the reminder that is enough becomes your light perfected.\n\nMore: Let whoever wills be mindful also parallels “So let whoever wills take the Right Way to your Lord” after this is a reminder, and the garden owners’ hope for a better garden after turning. Willing is not idle wish as with “each one wishes to be given a personal letter,” but active mindfulness as with being in awe without seeing whose mighty reward is forgiveness. Whoever wills indicates that the Reminder respects will, as with every soul detained for what it has done, and as with the Day they will be presented with no secret hidden. Willing now leads to the lead position among the warning, not lag, and to Gardens where you ask about the wicked with gratitude.",
  "56": "\"But they cannot do so unless Allah wills. He ˹alone˺ is worthy to be feared and entitled to forgive.\" completes the reminder invitation after “let whoever wills be mindful.” The verse balances human will and divine will.\n\n“But they cannot do so unless Allah wills. He alone is worthy to be feared and entitled to forgive” translates “wa ma yadhkuruna illa an yasha’a Allahu huwa ahlu al-taqwa wa ahlu al-maghfirah,” they will not be mindful except Allah wills; He is the One worthy of being feared and worthy to forgive. Worthy to be feared, or “ahlu al-taqwa,” is entitled to reverence, like the Lord of east and west, no god except Him, take Him as Trustee, and the One whose punishment none should feel secure from. Entitled to forgive, or “ahlu al-maghfirah,” is the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful who has accepted repentance, who will make a way out, provide from sources never imagined, be sufficient, and after hardship bring ease.\n\nUnless Allah wills shows that mindfulness is not self-generated, but enabled, as with “We will soon send upon you a weighty revelation” and the Pen’s writing, and as with “Allah determines the length of day and night, He knows you are unable to maintain it, so He has accepted your repentance, recite what is easy.”\n\nThis follows “let whoever wills be mindful” to show that willing itself is a gift, not a boast that honour expels inferior.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use “unless Allah wills” as excuse to not try, as with feeling secure that earth will not swallow. The Quran pairs it with “let whoever wills,” preserving both divine will and human effort.\n\nFor the believer, this verse is comfort. Worry not that mindfulness depends solely on your strength; it depends on the Worthy to be feared and entitled to forgive, who created death and life to test best deeds and is Almighty, All-Forgiving. Seek His will for mindfulness, turn in sincere repentance so light is perfected, and trust that He who is worthy will make you worthy to be among the people of the right, not detained, whose Gardens are where they ask about the wicked with gratitude, not as one of them."
};
